//! Zamani Quantum Frontend — OpenQASM Abstract Syntax Tree.
//!
//! This module contains the OpenQASM-specific syntactic representation.
//!
//! # Architectural boundary
//!
//! ```text
//! OpenQASM source
//!      │
//!      ▼
//!    lexer
//!      │
//!      ▼
//!  OpenQASM AST  ◄── this module
//!      │
//!      ▼
//! OpenQASM semantic validation
//!      │
//!      ▼
//! generic frontend lowering
//!      │
//!      ▼
//! Zamani Quantum IR
//! ```
//!
//! This module intentionally does NOT:
//!
//! - construct `QuantumCircuit`;
//! - construct canonical `Gate` values;
//! - perform OpenQASM semantic validation;
//! - resolve identifiers;
//! - resolve includes;
//! - access the filesystem;
//! - access the network;
//! - execute `extern` declarations;
//! - perform optimization;
//! - perform routing;
//! - perform scheduling;
//! - perform hardware mapping;
//! - perform backend execution.
//!
//! The AST represents OpenQASM syntax faithfully enough for later validation,
//! lowering, diagnostics, and (where supported) loss-aware export.
//!
//! # Design principles
//!
//! 1. OpenQASM has its own AST.
//! 2. The AST does not imitate the canonical Zamani Quantum IR.
//! 3. Source spans use the shared frontend source model.
//! 4. Unsupported constructs can be represented without silently discarding
//!    them.
//! 5. Semantic validation is intentionally separate from AST construction.
//! 6. Register operands are not reduced to individual qubits at parse time.
//! 7. Expressions preserve structure instead of being converted to `f64`.
//! 8. Gate modifiers are represented explicitly.
//! 9. Classical and quantum types are represented independently.
//! 10. Version information is preserved as data rather than hard-coded.
//!
//! # OpenQASM compatibility
//!
//! The structure follows the OpenQASM 3 grammar and leaves room for the
//! OpenQASM 3.0/3.1 language families, including:
//!
//! - version declarations;
//! - includes;
//! - annotations;
//! - pragmas;
//! - classical declarations;
//! - quantum declarations;
//! - aliases;
//! - gate definitions;
//! - gate calls;
//! - gate modifiers;
//! - measurement;
//! - reset;
//! - barriers;
//! - delays;
//! - boxes;
//! - assignments;
//! - expressions;
//! - `if`/`else`;
//! - `for`;
//! - `while`;
//! - `switch`;
//! - `break`;
//! - `continue`;
//! - `return`;
//! - subroutines (`def`);
//! - extern declarations;
//! - calibration grammar declarations;
//! - `cal` blocks;
//! - `defcal` blocks;
//! - old-style declarations;
//! - I/O declarations;
//! - timing types;
//! - physical qubits;
//! - slices and index sets;
//! - broadcasting operands.
//!
//! Constructs that the current Zamani IR cannot lower can still be represented
//! here and rejected later by validation/lowering with an explicit diagnostic.
//!
//! # Security
//!
//! This module performs no external I/O and does not execute source-language
//! constructs. Limits on source size, AST size, nesting depth, identifiers,
//! strings, and expressions are enforced by the parser/validation layers using
//! `FrontendLimits`.
//!
//! AST constructors perform local structural checks only. They do not replace
//! the format-specific semantic validator.
//!
//! # Rust compatibility
//!
//! Rust 2021.
//! Target toolchain: Rust 1.97.1.
//!
//! No nightly features are required.
//! No new dependencies are required.

